          "title": "You'll want to know: exsultō, exsultāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exsultō, exsultāre — \"to leap for joy, rejoice greatly\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puerī in hortō exsultant, quod hodiē fēriae sunt. The boys leap for joy in the garden, because today is a holiday."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exsultō, exsultāre, exsultāvī, exsultātum. First to leap up, then to be overjoyed. English exult."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exsultō is built on ex- \"out, up\" and saltō \"to jump, dance\", the frequentative of saliō \"to leap\" (Lewis & Short). English exult comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
